<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Let the derivative of <m>F(t)</m> be a given function <m>f</m>, so <m>\frac{ dF }{ dt } = f</m>. Then the function <m>F(t)</m>, whose derivative is <m>f</m>, is called an <term>antiderivative</term> of <m>f</m>.</p>
  
<p>Another name for the <term>antiderivative</term> <m>F(t)</m> is
  <answer name="ans1a">
    <choice>the <term>derivative</term> of <m>f(t)</m>, or <m>\frac{df}{dt}</m></choice>
    <choice credit="1">the <term>integral</term> of <m>f(t)</m>, or, <m>\int f(t) \, dt</m> </choice>
    <choice>the <term>linear approximation</term> of <m>f(t)</m></choice>
  </answer></p>
<hint>See <ref to="$v1">Key Moment 1</ref></hint>

</exercise>

<exercise includeAutoNumber="true"><title />
<p>Which function below, when differentiated, will give you the function <m>f(t) = t^3</m>? In other words, what is the <term>antiderivative</term> of <m>f</m>?
  <answer name="ans1b">
    <choice><m>F(t) = 3t^2 + C</m></choice>
    <choice credit="1"><m>F(t) = \frac{1}{4} t^4 + C</m></choice>
    <choice><m>F(t) = \frac{1}{2} t^2 + C</m></choice>
    <choice><m>F(t) = \frac{1}{3} t^4 + C</m></choice>
  </answer></p>
<hint>Try taking the derivative of each function <m>F(t)</m> to see if you get back <m>f</m> as a result. Remember, <m>C</m> is any constant, so its derivative is just 0. See <ref to="$v1">Key Moment 2</ref></hint>
<p>We can also write this <term>antiderivative</term> <m>F(t)</m> as the <term>integral</term> of <m>f(t)</m>. Which notation below is the correct way to express this?
  <answer name="ans1c">
    <choice><m>\int F(t) \, dt = f(t)</m></choice>
    <choice><m>F(t) = \sum f(t)</m></choice>
    <choice credit="1"><m>\int f(t) \, dt = F(t)</m></choice>
  </answer></p>
    
</exercise>
</problem>
</section>

